Bob LeChevalier (lojbab) wrote:
> At 10:37 AM 02/10/2000 +0200, Ivan A Derzhanski wrote:
> Since you would use "eda" in expressing "before/during/after eating",
> I would ask how Russians express eating/snacking that occurs between
> the customary meals of the day.

Well, there are words for `snack' (derived from `bite', mostly).
A trickier question is how one manages to exclude those when
talking of eating.  Context does the job: `3 times a day after
eating' clearly means `after the 3 major daily eatings'; `eat
_k_ times a day' is interpreted as `have _k_ meals a day'
precisely because snacks are accidental by nature.

